  <div class=3D"recipe-body-story-and-directions">
    <div class=3D"recipe-body-story">
            <p>When I was in high school, we would drive an hour to Berkele=
y for concerts, nose rings, vintage shopping on Telegraph Avenue, and <em>t=
hat </em>salad. It was a colorful, fully loaded leafy salad we all loved, a=
nd it would power our day. Crunchy lettuces, mixed beans, seeds, carrot coi=
ns, and croutons were all staples, with a creamy, but not-too-creamy, zesty=
 dressing. If you=E2=80=99re looking for a meal-size salad, this is it. And=
, by all means, if you have radicchio, add some of that as well. This is gr=
eat with the orange-tahini dressing included here.</p>

          </div>

    <div class=3D"recipe-body-directions">

              <h3 class=3D"simple-h3">Directions</h3>
     =20
      <div class=3D"recipe-body-list-container">
        <ol>
                                      <li>
                  Shred the lettuce, or leave it intact, and place it in a =
large bowl. Add the tomatoes, beans, chickpeas, celery, carrot, and crouton=
s.
                                  </li>
                                                  <li>
                  In a small bowl, whisk together the tahini, orange juice,=
 ponzu, and honey.
                                  </li>
                                                  <li>
                  Toss the salad well with as much or as little of the dres=
sing as you like; any leftover dressing can be stored in an airtight contai=
ner in the refrigerator for up to 5 days.
                                  </li>
                                                  <li>
                  Serve the salad family-style, topped with the sunflower s=
eeds.
                                  </li>
                              </ol>
      </div>

